1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:06,960
Clocks drift. Logs from two servers disagree by a minute, a certificate says it is not valid yet,

2
00:00:06,980 --> 00:00:10,352
a login fails. The cause is often just wrong time.

3
00:00:10,372 --> 00:00:13,201
One small service keeps every clock right.

4
00:00:13,671 --> 00:00:16,403
In this video: configure time service clients.

5
00:00:20,923 --> 00:00:23,506
Here's the task, the way an exam would state it.

6
00:00:23,976 --> 00:00:25,769
Configure time service clients.

7
00:00:26,239 --> 00:00:29,344
We'll look at the client configuration, add a time server,

8
00:00:29,364 --> 00:00:31,552
and check that the clock is synchronized.

9
00:00:38,072 --> 00:00:43,831
A time client asks time servers for the correct time, and adjusts the local clock.

10
00:00:43,851 --> 00:00:45,875
On RHEL the client is chrony.

11
00:00:45,895 --> 00:00:51,442
Its service is chrony D, its configuration file is slash E T C slash chrony dot

12
00:00:51,462 --> 00:00:53,839
conf, and its command is chrony C.

13
00:01:00,309 --> 00:01:01,809
The protocol is N T P.

14
00:01:01,829 --> 00:01:06,164
In the configuration file, a server line names one time server.

15
00:01:06,184 --> 00:01:09,690
A pool line names a group of servers. Iburst means:

16
00:01:09,710 --> 00:01:14,390
send a quick burst of requests at start, so the clock is set sooner.

17
00:01:21,860 --> 00:01:25,755
Before the demo, silently, the lab installs the chrony package.

18
00:01:25,775 --> 00:01:27,557
This step changes the system.

19
00:01:35,077 --> 00:01:39,967
First, the configuration. Grep prints the lines that match. The caret means:

20
00:01:39,987 --> 00:01:43,973
at the start of the line. So we see every pool or server line.

21
00:01:43,993 --> 00:01:46,428
This machine uses a pool, with iburst.

22
00:01:54,898 --> 00:01:55,988
Now add a server.

23
00:01:56,008 --> 00:01:59,385
Echo prints the line, the pipe hands it to sudo tee,

24
00:01:59,405 --> 00:02:04,350
and tee dash a appends it to the end of the file. Grep shows both lines now.

25
00:02:13,870 --> 00:02:15,046
Next, the service.

26
00:02:15,066 --> 00:02:20,227
Is dash enabled says it starts at boot, and is dash active says it is running.

27
00:02:20,247 --> 00:02:25,541
Chrony D reads its configuration only at start, so after a change we restart it.

28
00:02:36,061 --> 00:02:38,495
Now check. Sleep twenty gives it time.

29
00:02:38,515 --> 00:02:43,725
Then chrony C sources lists the time servers it uses. Each line shows one server:

30
00:02:43,745 --> 00:02:48,052
its name, its stratum, how far away it is from the reference clock,

31
00:02:48,072 --> 00:02:50,441
and the measured offset of our clock.

32
00:03:00,911 --> 00:03:03,912
The star marks the server we are synchronized to.

33
00:03:03,932 --> 00:03:07,056
A dash means a good server, not selected right now.

34
00:03:14,576 --> 00:03:20,267
Time date control gives the overview. System clock synchronized: yes. N T P service:

35
00:03:20,287 --> 00:03:24,007
active. The clock is right, and the service is working.

36
00:03:33,527 --> 00:03:36,963
Our five steps: task, demo, check, reboot, and your turn.

37
00:03:38,483 --> 00:03:41,145
We reboot. Chrony D is enabled and active.

38
00:03:41,165 --> 00:03:44,019
The sources show question marks for a moment:

39
00:03:44,039 --> 00:03:48,490
after a boot, chrony measures the servers again before it chooses one.

40
00:03:59,010 --> 00:04:00,542
Your turn. Add the line:

41
00:04:00,562 --> 00:04:05,003
server, ntp dot lab dot example, iburst, to the chrony configuration.

42
00:04:05,023 --> 00:04:07,589
Make chrony D start at boot and run now.

43
00:04:07,609 --> 00:04:11,468
The server name does not exist, so nothing will synchronize.

44
00:04:11,488 --> 00:04:15,865
The checker only looks at the line and the service. Run the checker.

45
00:04:24,335 --> 00:04:26,085
Try before you open the solution.

46
00:04:31,605 --> 00:04:37,253
Next up: install and update software packages from Red Hat Content Delivery Network,

47
00:04:37,273 --> 00:04:40,694
a remote repository, or from the local file system.

48
00:04:41,164 --> 00:04:44,067
Subscribe to follow the series, and let's keep going.
